It's cheaper.

>>...To be more precise, what anarchists object
>>to is the State, defined as a territorial monopoly on
>>legitimate violence.
>
>OK.  Suppose I did for some reason move into one of these
>'proprietary communities' you are talking about.  Wouldn't the
>agency responsible for law in that community have a territorial
>monopoly on legitimate violence?  And wouldn't that make it a
>State in the sense you are defining here?

Why should there be only one justice agency?  So long as they all
go after "child abusers" the same way, there can be as many
different justice agencies as the client base could financially
support.  There could even be specialization within the field of
fighting child abuse by different agencies.

To answer your question a bit more directly, supposing that people
did move into a community with one justice agency, that would be a
natural monopoly.  Natural monopolies are highly unstable, and thus
rare.  They tend to either become coercive, or lose market share to
competitive entry.

Anarco-capitalists don't object to natural monopolies (except insofar
as they're unstable and may become coercive), just to coercive ones.

>From: dasher@netcom.com (Anton Sherwood)
