>Robin says:
>> My understanding is that federal law prohibited national branch
>> banking.  This caused, among other problems, a big difference in
>> interest rates in the west vs. the east, slowing growth in the west.
>> A national branch bank would have had notes issued in Calif and
>> accepted in Kansas.
>
>When did that law change?  I have the impression that if it's not 
>still around, traces of it are, and that First Interstate Bank had 
>to dodge around the law to function.

That changed during the Civil War when the National Banking Act was
passed.  Nationally-chartered banks were allowed to open branches in
more than one state.  That was also when banknotes were nationalized,
resulting in greater profit opportunities for counterfeiters since
they only had one pattern to copy and there was less redemption of
notes (no redemption at all during the Civil War itself).  This led
to the creation of the Secret Service within the Department of the
Treasury.

So, currency nationalization actually led to more forgery, not less.
According to an econ grad student studying free banking that I know,
there wasn't much forgery during the U.S. free banking period.

>I suppose interest rates equalized through indirect arbitrage?

I dunno.

