From: William_John@gnj.or.jp (William John)
Date: Sun,  4 Dec 94 04:56:18 +0900
Subject: [#94-12-110] Environment, Markets, Private Cities

I thought I might share my viewpoint based on my experience of living in Tokyo,
a city which is much more privatized than any city I've ever visited in the
United States.  Tokyo has virtually no public space.  (To give you some idea, I
once saw a statistic that said Tokyo only has 10% as much park space per capita
as New York City, which is not exactly a garden of green growing things itself.)
Unless you are lucky enough to be near one of the few parks wedged in among the
concrete, you must pay someone just to sit down.  There is no place to sit
outside at all (no profit in that for anyone).   You can get really tired trying
to find a cup of coffee for less than five dollars just to get off your feet for
a minute.

Basically, Tokyo has few zoning rules and very little allocation of public
space.  Thus, everything has been paved over, built up, and has an admission
fee.  Would YOU like this kind of development plan in your community?

(BTW, Tokyo does have a lot going for it, and I enjoy living here.  However, I'd
enjoy it even more if there were a few green trees here and there.  No profit in
planting trees, I guess.  It's cheaper to put plastic trees inside your
building, along with walls of TV screens playing videotapes [w/sound] of rivers
and waterfalls, as I have seen at some department stores here.)

