Then I guess you're willing to join me in one of the Audubon Wildlife
Refuges near here, to drain it and put in trailer parks? After all,
it's already useless as a wildlife refuge, what with the drilling rigs
and all that.... so we might as well get our money's worth.

Oh, and ignore the Pelicans and other birds; they must be your imagination.
After all, everyone knows that no life is possible near a drilling rig...

In our state we're going through an "overzealousness" phase of environmental
enforcement (oddly enough, at the same time as a "laxness" phase: everyone
knows that the six phases of any project are enthusiasm, disillusionment, 
panic, search for the guilty, punishment of innocent, and praise and honors for
non-participants), but with that caveat: it _is_ possible to extract oil
(well, maybe not; after all I think but don't remember for sure that they
did not find oil in the refuge: they did find natural gas, although one
is typically present with the other) without harming the environment much.

I'm not sure the company that gets the contract either by lowest bid
or by bribing the governor will be able to accomplish it, which might
be why nonprofits seem to be able to do resource extraction and wilderness
protection at the same time, while governments can't.

This is all stuff we should be considering as we take in the dual facts
that for the past couple decades the environemntal movement has championed
wilderness protection by classification as public resources, and that
due to recent political developments it seems certian that drilling
may start in the Arctic Wildlife Refuge in the near future with a lot
less safeguards than the Audobon (gee, I think I misspelled Audobon
somewhere up there, or maybe down here) Society ENFORCED on those
conducting activities on its land.


