And as I said, there is no guarantee that anarchy will lead to a
libertarian society but there is reason for optimism. It's easy
to vote for some idiot politician who says he will stop your
neighbor from sinning, it's much harder to shell out money for
it. For example, in this democracy many towns have laws against
women wearing string bikinis on the beach and it's simple to see
why, there are plenty of prudes and it doesn't cost them
anything to vote. Now let's consider this in Friedman's world.
The prude is considering 2 Private Protection Agencies (PPA) 
that are identical except for one thing, one has a decency
patrol to harass women on the beach 20 miles from your home, the
other doesn't but uses the money saved to send an extra security
patrol through your neighborhood at 3Am. The prude would have to
be very prudish indeed to pick the first PPA. 
     
	    >the private security firms would handle the disposition of
	    >criminal cases -- they would try the perps if there were trials;
	    >imprison them if there were prisons; execute them if there was 
	    >capital punishment.
	     
NO, that's not Friedman's  idea or anybody else's that I know
of. He would be tried by an independent arbitrator picked by
BOTH sides. The arbitrator would be paid by the case not by the
year, those who gained a reputation for fairness would get a lot
of business ( remember both sides picked him) those who favored
one side or made stupid or brutal decisions would not be picked
again and be looking for a new line of work. Unlike present  day
judges and juries ,justice would have a positive survival value
for the arbitrator.
		  
	   > a quick sidewalk interrogation and then either a memorable
	   >beating or a bullet to the brain, [...]  Such a service would be
	   >able to underbid dramatically any agency that
	   >promised such frills as full trials, imprisonment, an appeals
	   >process, and the like.
	   
No they wouldn't because the huge army they would need would be
very expensive. The other PPA's would be unhappy about having
their clients beaten or killed, it's bad for business.
	   
	 >Suppose they *hadn't* so contracted?  Suppose they had
	 >missed the last payment? 
	  
Suppose they don't spend money for food? Better look for some charity.
	  
	>Suppose they were gambling that they could get away without
	>paying the monthly fee to their agency, like people running        
	>without health insurance?
	
Then that's their choice, they may win their gamble, they may
not. I don't think it's my duty to make the world safe for fools.
	
	>Suppose they had an agency, but it was a cheapie contract
